                        text "Applied only tuning supported by controlled interleaved measurements and compiler instrumentation. A release-profile matrix tested ThinLTO, one codegen unit, their combination, and fat LTO against the unchanged polished binary; codegen-units=1 alone was neutral, while fat LTO improved graph-heavy medians and reduced loaded binary sections from 1,112,121 to 1,030,160 bytes, so only lto=\"fat\" was retained. LLVM profile instrumentation over all six workloads identified scanning, canonicalization, mknode, and variable evaluation as the highest-count paths. Replacing Graph's ordered node-path index with the standard randomized HashMap and avoiding hit-path key allocation improved the graph-heavy workloads by roughly 2–11% in a 21-sample interleaved comparison. Observable order remains arena-defined, and RandomState preserves collision-attack resistance for manifest-controlled paths. Broader environment/edge HashMap experiments were neutral overall and regressed some workloads, so they were removed. PGO, platform-specific flags, buffer changes, and codegen-units=1 were not retained without portable evidence. On the exact final tree, the 15-sample release gate passed at 0.42–0.86x pinned Ninja runtime and 0.73–1.31x Ninja peak RSS. Strict Clippy/rustdoc, all-target Rust tests, all 425 pinned Ninja tests, 10 differential cases, persistence round trips, and wave-4 coverage for all 175 symbols pass."
                    }

